{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact20db5639feb8d3177208a3af0f3bc98e94958809, primary/all1artifact2e76e3a3. This reviews the documentation direction only; no implementation is commissioned.\n\nF1 [P2, Architecture] Reconcile content identities throughout sections2–4/7 with decision1 (lines23–24,47–50,61–62, manifest table, gap1 versus257–262). The earlier text says pack creates one content ID for the whole closure and install/grants/replay use “the content id”; decision1 expressly puts screens outside the semantic ID and permits presentation updates without reinstall. These describe different identity boundaries. State which IDs cover semantics, presentation and any distribution envelope, what an install/grant pins, and how an update is authorized/recorded without altering old replay. Preserve Hugh's split-ID decision; do not silently hash presentation back into semantic identity. A reader should be able to predict which IDs change for a screen-only edit versus a capability change.\n\nF2 [P2, Security] “No server code” plus the lending CSP is not a browser isolation or signing boundary (lines55–60, generic route insection5, decision1). Today's lending route is trusted same-origin JavaScript (scope/src/lending.ts), with script-src/connect-src self; lending/browser/storage.ts stores device CryptoKeys and kept joins in origin-local IndexedDB and join.ts makes a signer from them. A new path under that origin does not isolate package JavaScript from that custody. Non-extractable keys prevent export, not use by code holding a CryptoKey. A package's replacement presentation could therefore reach more than its projection or request signatures outside its intended application unless the host mediates it. Inference from these source boundaries and the browser origin model: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Defenses/Same-origin_policy and https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/script-src .\n\nName the presentation trust model and required host-controlled read/signing/custody boundary before installing arbitrary packages. This can be a declared prerequisite rather than a complete sandbox design here. State how a presentation-only release is admitted, who authorizes it, and that it cannot inherit ambient room/admin signing power. Reconcile M1 with these prerequisites: M1 installs packages generically while projections and bounded capability grants arrive only inM2. Trusted fixture-only M1 can be labelled as intermediate evidence, but cannot claim the unrestricted destination complete. The safety claim must rest on actual enforced boundaries, not static assets/CSP alone.\n\nF3 [P2, Architecture] Strengthen upgrade and milestone completion conditions (gap10; M3 lines243–245; M4 lines247–249). Copying settled state to a linked new scope while both journals replay does not prevent both old and new scopes exercising the same right, or pending/unknown outside work being reissued. Name an authorized cutoff/handoff, treatment of old write authority and pending/unknown operations, and preservation of immutable source provenance as M3 obligations. Its witness should distinguish duplicated authority/work from a valid carry-forward, not merely replay two independently valid journals. M4 has no done condition; give the observable exact-boundary upgrade/no-backfill/stale-act/refusal-and-replay witness, while leaving its design/research explicitly owed. These are design obligations, not a request to implement them now.\n\nF4 [P2, factual attribution] Correct two current/source claims. Section2 lines36–38 attributes provision of the record to the Weixin host, but cited survey sections1–3 says cloud development OR developer-owned backend, with logic/data/record owned by each developer. Separate the useful host identity/joining/notification analogy from the proposed dap record-host duty. Gap10 step1 says successor declarations name a predecessor and “the runtime supports this today,” then says no lineage is recorded. Current dap supports new definitions for new scopes while old scopes retain their pins (write-an-application.md), not the proposed predecessor declaration/upgrade lineage. Say precisely that coexistence is implemented and declared lineage is owed.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: the unit/package, per-room install and incremental upgrade direction matches recorded requestfd0cc986 and dap-spike sections3/6/7, but F1/F3 prevent approval. Security conclusion: current grants and replay remain explicitly preserved, and later server-code escape hatch is separated correctly; browser presentation needs F2 before the note can claim safe generic install. Simplification conclusion: one SDK, one route and one authored package reduce existing duplication; keep that structure and correct F4/ambiguous IDs rather than adding another application framework.\n\nAll five decisions match the explicit owner-attributed requestfd0cc986 I could inspect; I did not independently see Hugh's originating chat. Checked stated dap baselinebc95110e, the single-file native diff, application guide/pins/provisioning/publish-file restriction, CLI cohort/description claims, MCP generic derivation, roadmap limits/milestones, lending shelf/storage/join/route, example browser structure and runbook364lines; consolidationacb0f6b8 dispatch still composes app routes. Read exact dap-spike9d738e2e design sections3/6/7 and Weixin survey sections1–3. No project execution or gate, per the documentation-only invitation. No findings assert that these proposed features already run.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2e76e3a322cab3f723e5175dcf82dd57eddd6d32","binding":"assigned","head":"20db5639feb8d3177208a3af0f3bc98e94958809","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:73c242b1867922fec2f2e0764223454190d3ece7\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git